================================================================
LESSON 1: SOCIAL MEDIA CONTENT CREATOR
================================================================

WHAT YOU'LL DO
--------------
Create posts, captions, and simple graphics for small businesses.
Businesses need someone to keep their social media active and looking
good — and they'll pay you to do it.

----------------------------------------------------------------
SECTION 1: WHAT IS SOCIAL MEDIA MANAGEMENT?
----------------------------------------------------------------
Social media management means running a business's social media accounts
on their behalf. That can include writing captions, designing graphics,
posting content, and sometimes responding to comments.

Businesses hire people for this because they're too busy running their
actual business to think about Instagram. Your job is to handle that
for them so their page stays active and attractive to customers.

----------------------------------------------------------------
SECTION 2: PICKING YOUR PLATFORM
----------------------------------------------------------------
Don't try to master every platform at once. Pick one to start:

  Instagram  — Great for visual businesses like food, fashion, fitness,
               and beauty. Posts, Stories, and Reels are the main formats.

  TikTok     — Short-form video content. Best for younger audiences and
               businesses that want to go viral. More creative and casual.

  Facebook   — Older audience. Good for local businesses like restaurants,
               shops, and services. Posts and events work well here.

Start with whichever platform you use most yourself — you'll already
understand how it works.

----------------------------------------------------------------
SECTION 3: WHAT CLIENTS ACTUALLY WANT FROM YOU
----------------------------------------------------------------
Most small business clients want three things:

  1. Consistency — posting regularly (3-5 times per week) so their
     page doesn't go silent.

  2. Professionalism — posts that look clean and on-brand, not like
     they were made in five seconds.

  3. Engagement — content that gets likes, comments, and follows.
     This usually means relatable captions, good visuals, and using
     hashtags effectively.

They don't usually expect you to go viral. They just want their page
to look active and trustworthy.

----------------------------------------------------------------
SECTION 4: HOW TO WRITE A GOOD CAPTION
----------------------------------------------------------------
A good caption does three things:

  1. Grabs attention in the first line (most platforms cut off after
     1-2 lines, so the opener has to hook people).

  2. Delivers value or personality — share a tip, a story, a question,
     or something fun related to the business.

  3. Ends with a call to action — tell people what to do next:
     "Comment below," "Save this for later," "Click the link in bio."

Example for a bakery:
  Bad caption:   "Fresh bread today!"
  Good caption:  "Your Monday just got better. Our sourdough just came
                  out of the oven — golden crust, soft inside, best
                  eaten warm. Come grab a loaf before they're gone.
                  Open until 6pm. [bakery location]"

----------------------------------------------------------------
SECTION 5: FREE TOOLS — CANVA AND LATER
----------------------------------------------------------------
Canva (canva.com)
  Free graphic design tool. Use it to make posts, stories, and banners.
  It has templates for every social platform size so you don't have to
  guess dimensions. Search for the platform (e.g., "Instagram post") and
  pick a template to customize.

Later (later.com)
  Free scheduling tool. You design your posts, then schedule them to go
  live automatically at the best times. This saves you from having to
  manually post every day.

Both have free plans that are more than enough to get started.

----------------------------------------------------------------
SECTION 6: HOW TO FIND YOUR FIRST CLIENT
----------------------------------------------------------------
Your first client is almost always someone you already know. Try:

  - Local businesses in your neighborhood (cafes, salons, gyms)
  - A family member's business
  - A small business you already follow online that has a weak page

How to approach them:
  Don't just say "I do social media." Show them. Before you reach out,
  make 3 sample posts for their business using Canva. Then message or
  walk in and say something like:

  "Hey, I noticed your Instagram hasn't been updated in a while.
   I made a few sample posts for you — want to take a look? I'm
   offering social media management at a starter rate."

A portfolio of samples (even fake ones) beats a pitch every time.

----------------------------------------------------------------
SECTION 7: WHAT TO CHARGE
----------------------------------------------------------------
Starting rates (adjust as you gain experience):

  Per post:         $5–$15 per post
  Monthly package:  $50–$150/month for 3 posts per week
  With graphics:    Add $5–$10 per designed graphic

Be upfront about what's included. A clear agreement avoids confusion:
  "I'll create and post 12 posts per month, including captions and
   graphics, for $100/month."

----------------------------------------------------------------
MINI PROJECT
----------------------------------------------------------------
Create a 3-post sample for a fake business of your choice (e.g.,
a pet grooming shop, a small bakery, or a local gym).

For each post:
  - Design a graphic in Canva
  - Write a caption with a hook, body, and call to action
  - Add 5-10 relevant hashtags

Save these as your portfolio samples to show future clients.

