================================================================
LESSON 2: LOGO & GRAPHIC DESIGN
================================================================

WHAT YOU'LL DO
--------------
Design simple logos, flyers, and banners using free tools. Businesses
always need visual assets — and good-looking design is something
many of them will happily pay for.

----------------------------------------------------------------
SECTION 1: WHAT GRAPHIC DESIGN FREELANCING LOOKS LIKE
----------------------------------------------------------------
As a freelance graphic designer, clients will ask you to create things
like:

  - Logos (the visual symbol/name of their brand)
  - Flyers (for events, sales, or promotions)
  - Social media banners or profile images
  - Business cards
  - Email headers or website graphics

You're not expected to be a professional designer. You're expected to
produce clean, consistent, usable visuals. With the right tools and a
little practice, that's very achievable.

----------------------------------------------------------------
SECTION 2: INTRODUCTION TO CANVA
----------------------------------------------------------------
Canva (canva.com) is your main tool. It's free, browser-based, and
beginner-friendly.

Key features to learn:
  - Templates — pre-built designs you can customize
  - Brand Kit — save a color palette, fonts, and logo for a client
  - Elements — shapes, icons, lines, and illustrations
  - Text — add and style fonts
  - Upload — add your own images or a client's photos/logo

Start by clicking around the free templates. Search for "logo,"
"flyer," or "business card" to see what's available. The goal is
to customize templates to match a brand — not to start from scratch.

----------------------------------------------------------------
SECTION 3: BASIC DESIGN RULES
----------------------------------------------------------------
Three principles cover 90% of good beginner design:

  Colors
    Stick to 2-3 colors maximum per design. Use a main color,
    an accent color, and usually a neutral (white, black, or gray).
    Colors should feel consistent with the brand's personality
    (e.g., blue for trust, green for health, red for energy).

  Fonts
    Use no more than 2 fonts per design. One for headings (bold
    and eye-catching) and one for body text (clean and readable).
    Mixing too many fonts looks messy.

  Spacing
    Give elements room to breathe. Avoid cramming too much into
    one design. White space (empty space) makes designs look
    more professional, not less.

----------------------------------------------------------------
SECTION 4: WHAT FILES TO SEND YOUR CLIENT
----------------------------------------------------------------
Different clients need different file types:

  PNG   — For digital use (social media, websites). Supports
          transparent backgrounds. This is what you'll send most often.

  PDF   — For printing (flyers, business cards). High quality
          and keeps formatting intact.

  JPG   — For photos or designs without transparency. Smaller
          file size than PNG.

Always ask the client what they'll use the file for before you
deliver it so you send the right format.

In Canva, click "Share" → "Download" → choose your file type.

----------------------------------------------------------------
SECTION 5: HOW TO PRICE YOUR WORK
----------------------------------------------------------------
Starting rates:

  Logo design:      $10–$50 (simple, 2-3 concepts, 1 revision)
  Flyer design:     $10–$30
  Social banner:    $5–$20
  Business card:    $10–$25

Tips:
  - Offer 1 revision included, charge extra for more
  - Charge more if they want multiple file formats
  - As your portfolio grows, raise your prices

----------------------------------------------------------------
SECTION 6: WHERE TO SELL YOUR WORK
----------------------------------------------------------------
  Fiverr         — Create a gig listing for logo design. Clients
                   search and hire you directly.

  School/local   — Offer to design for school clubs, events,
                   or local small businesses.

  Local shops    — Walk in with samples. Restaurants, hair salons,
                   and small retail stores are great targets.

----------------------------------------------------------------
MINI PROJECT
----------------------------------------------------------------
Design a logo for a made-up brand. Choose a business type you find
interesting (e.g., a food truck, a dog walking service, a gaming
channel).

Your logo should include:
  - The business name (as text)
  - A simple icon or graphic element
  - A color scheme of 2-3 colors
  - Clean, readable font choices

Export as PNG with a transparent background. This is your first
portfolio piece.

