LESSON 3: VIDEO EDITING
================================================================

WHAT YOU'LL DO
--------------
Edit short videos for YouTubers, TikTokers, or small businesses.
Raw footage needs to be cut, polished, and formatted before it's
ready to publish — that's your job.

----------------------------------------------------------------
SECTION 1: WHAT VIDEO EDITING GIGS LOOK LIKE
----------------------------------------------------------------
Clients give you raw footage (unedited video they've recorded) and
you turn it into a finished video. That usually means:

  - Cutting out mistakes, pauses, and filler words
  - Adding background music
  - Adding text or captions
  - Adding intro/outro sequences
  - Color correcting the footage
  - Exporting in the right format and resolution

Clients who hire video editors include YouTubers, TikTok creators,
small businesses making ads, real estate agents, and podcasters
who want video versions of their episodes.

----------------------------------------------------------------
SECTION 2: FREE EDITING TOOLS
----------------------------------------------------------------
CapCut (capcut.com or mobile app)
  Perfect for short-form content (TikTok, Reels, YouTube Shorts).
  Very beginner-friendly. Has automatic captions, templates, effects,
  and transitions built in. Free to use.

DaVinci Resolve (blackmagicdesign.com)
  Professional-grade editor available for free. Steeper learning curve
  but far more powerful. Best for longer videos, YouTube content,
  or clients who want more polished results.

Start with CapCut if you're new. Move to DaVinci Resolve when you're
ready to level up.

----------------------------------------------------------------
SECTION 3: BASIC EDITS YOU NEED TO KNOW
----------------------------------------------------------------
These are the skills every video editing client expects:

  Cutting clips
    Trim out mistakes, dead air, and unwanted footage. The edit
    should feel smooth — like there were no mistakes to begin with.

  Adding music
    Background music sets the tone. Keep it quieter than the
    speaker's voice. Use royalty-free music from sites like
    Pixabay or YouTube Audio Library so there are no copyright issues.

  Adding captions
    Many viewers watch without sound. Captions make videos more
    accessible and keep people watching longer. CapCut can
    auto-generate captions — you just proofread and correct them.

----------------------------------------------------------------
SECTION 4: HOW TO EXPORT VIDEOS IN THE RIGHT FORMAT
----------------------------------------------------------------
Different platforms need different export settings:

  YouTube (standard)    — 1080p, MP4, H.264 codec, 30fps
  TikTok / Reels        — 1080 x 1920 (vertical), MP4, 30fps
  YouTube Shorts        — Same as TikTok (vertical)
  Business ads          — Ask the client for specs, or default to
                          1080p MP4 which works almost everywhere

Always export in the highest quality the platform supports, and
confirm the file plays correctly before delivering it.

----------------------------------------------------------------
SECTION 5: WHAT CLIENTS NEED
----------------------------------------------------------------
  YouTube intros/outros  — 5–15 second branded sequences
  Short-form clips       — TikTok, Reels, YouTube Shorts (60–90 sec)
  Ads                    — 15–30 second promotional videos
  Full YouTube videos    — 5–20 minute edited episodes
  Podcast video clips    — 60-second highlight clips from long episodes

Ask the client before you start: What platform is this for? What
length do you want? Do you have a style reference?

----------------------------------------------------------------
SECTION 6: HOW TO PRICE YOUR WORK
----------------------------------------------------------------
Starting rates:

  Short-form video (under 2 min):   $15–$30 per video
  YouTube video (5–15 min):         $30–$75 per video
  Ad/promo clip:                    $20–$50
  Ongoing work (weekly videos):     Offer a monthly rate for
                                    discounted, predictable income

----------------------------------------------------------------
MINI PROJECT
----------------------------------------------------------------
Edit a 60-second video from raw clips. You can film yourself
explaining something, ask a friend to be your subject, or use
free stock footage from sites like Pexels.

Your edit should include:
  - Cuts to remove any pauses or mistakes
  - Background music at an appropriate volume
  - At least 3 lines of captions
  - A clean opening and ending

Export and watch the full video before calling it done.

