LESSON 4: COPYWRITING & BLOG WRITING
================================================================

WHAT YOU'LL DO
--------------
Write articles, product descriptions, or website text for businesses.
Words are one of the most valuable things a business can have —
and most business owners don't enjoy writing them.

----------------------------------------------------------------
SECTION 1: WHAT COPYWRITING IS (AND WHY BUSINESSES PAY FOR IT)
----------------------------------------------------------------
Copywriting is writing text that serves a purpose for a business.
That purpose is usually to inform, persuade, or sell.

Examples of copy:
  - A product description on a website
  - A blog post that answers a customer's question
  - A homepage that explains what the business does
  - An email newsletter sent to subscribers
  - A social media caption

Businesses pay for good writing because bad writing loses customers.
If a product description is confusing or boring, people won't buy.
If a blog post is helpful and clear, it builds trust and brings in
search traffic.

----------------------------------------------------------------
SECTION 2: WRITING FOR THE WEB VS. WRITING FOR SCHOOL
----------------------------------------------------------------
Web writing and school writing are very different:

  School writing          Web writing
  --------------------    --------------------
  Long paragraphs         Short paragraphs (2-3 lines max)
  Formal tone             Conversational tone
  Complex vocabulary      Simple, clear words
  No subheadings          Lots of headings and structure
  Written to impress      Written to be useful

On the web, people scan before they read. Structure your content
so someone skimming can get the main points from headings and
the first sentence of each paragraph.

----------------------------------------------------------------
SECTION 3: HOW TO STRUCTURE A BLOG POST
----------------------------------------------------------------
A standard blog post structure:

  1. Headline     — Grabs attention and tells the reader exactly
                    what they'll learn. Example: "5 Easy Ways to
                    Save Money on Groceries This Week"

  2. Introduction — 2-3 sentences that confirm the reader is in
                    the right place and set up what you'll cover.

  3. Subheadings  — Break the article into clear sections with
                    descriptive headings (H2s).

  4. Body         — Each section answers one part of the topic.
                    Keep sentences short. Use examples.

  5. Conclusion   — Summarize the key points and tell the reader
                    what to do next (read another post, buy something,
                    sign up for a newsletter).

----------------------------------------------------------------
SECTION 4: USING SIMPLE SEO — KEYWORDS
----------------------------------------------------------------
SEO stands for Search Engine Optimization. It's the practice of
writing content that shows up on Google when people search.

The basics for beginners:

  What is a keyword?
    A word or phrase people type into Google. Example: someone
    searching for a plumber might type "affordable plumber near me."

  Why do keywords matter?
    If you write a blog post about "best running shoes for beginners"
    and include that phrase naturally throughout the article, Google
    is more likely to show it to someone who searches that phrase.

  How to use keywords:
    - Include the main keyword in the headline
    - Mention it a few times in the article (but don't stuff it in
      everywhere — write for humans first)
    - Use it in at least one subheading if it makes sense

You don't need special tools to start. Just think: what would
someone type into Google to find this article?

----------------------------------------------------------------
SECTION 5: FREE TOOLS
----------------------------------------------------------------
Google Docs
  Write and format your articles here. Easy to share a link
  with clients for review without sending attachments.

Grammarly (grammarly.com)
  Free grammar and spelling checker. Paste your text in and
  it flags errors and suggests improvements. Use it before
  you deliver any piece of writing.

----------------------------------------------------------------
SECTION 6: HOW TO PRICE YOUR WORK
----------------------------------------------------------------
Starting rates:

  Short blog post (300–500 words):       $5–$15
  Standard blog post (500–800 words):    $15–$35
  Long article (1,000+ words):           $30–$75
  Product description (100–150 words):   $5–$15 each

As you build a portfolio and get faster, raise your rates.
Experienced copywriters charge $0.10–$0.25+ per word.

----------------------------------------------------------------
SECTION 7: WHERE TO FIND WORK
----------------------------------------------------------------
  Fiverr     — Create a writing gig. Be specific ("I will write
               SEO blog posts for small businesses").

  Upwork     — Apply to writing jobs posted by businesses.

  Local      — Offer to write website content or blog posts for
               businesses near you. Many small businesses have
               outdated or badly written websites.

----------------------------------------------------------------
MINI PROJECT
----------------------------------------------------------------
Write a 300-word blog post on any topic you know well.

Requirements:
  - A clear, specific headline
  - An introduction (2-3 sentences)
  - At least 2 subheadings
  - Short paragraphs (2-3 lines max)
  - A conclusion with a clear closing statement

Run it through Grammarly before considering it finished.

