LESSON 6: DATA ENTRY & ONLINE RESEARCH
================================================================

WHAT YOU'LL DO
--------------
Help businesses organize information, build spreadsheets, or research
topics online. This gig requires less creative skill than others —
but it requires careful attention to detail and accuracy.

----------------------------------------------------------------
SECTION 1: WHAT DATA ENTRY AND RESEARCH GIGS ARE
----------------------------------------------------------------
Data entry means taking information from one source and putting it
into a structured format — usually a spreadsheet or database.

Examples:
  - Typing a list of customer names and emails into a spreadsheet
  - Entering sales numbers from paper receipts into Google Sheets
  - Building a contact list from a website's directory page
  - Organizing product information from a supplier catalog

Online research means finding specific information for a business.

Examples:
  - Finding the contact email for 50 businesses in a certain city
  - Researching competitor prices for a product
  - Finding statistics or sources for a report someone is writing
  - Compiling a list of events in a specific industry

These tasks are time-consuming and repetitive, which is exactly why
businesses pay someone else to do them.

----------------------------------------------------------------
SECTION 2: TOOLS YOU NEED
----------------------------------------------------------------
Google Sheets (free, browser-based)
  The main tool for data entry work. Learn how to:
  - Create and name columns
  - Freeze the top row (so headers stay visible when scrolling)
  - Sort and filter data
  - Use basic formulas: SUM, AVERAGE, VLOOKUP (optional but useful)

Microsoft Excel (paid, but similar to Sheets)
  Some clients will send you Excel files (.xlsx). Google Sheets
  can open and edit Excel files, so you don't need to buy Excel.

----------------------------------------------------------------
SECTION 3: HOW TO ORGANIZE DATA NEATLY
----------------------------------------------------------------
Good data entry follows simple rules:

  Consistent formatting
    Dates always in the same format (e.g., 01/15/2025 not "Jan 15").
    Phone numbers always in the same format (e.g., 555-123-4567).
    Names always capitalized correctly (not "john smith" or "JOHN SMITH").

  One piece of information per cell
    Don't put first and last name in the same cell if they'll need
    to be sorted separately. One cell per distinct piece of data.

  Clear column headers
    Label every column. "Email," "Phone," "City," "Date Added"
    — each column should have a clear header in the first row.

  No blank rows in the middle
    Blank rows break sorting and filters. Keep the data continuous.

----------------------------------------------------------------
SECTION 4: HOW TO DO ONLINE RESEARCH EFFICIENTLY
----------------------------------------------------------------
Use Google search effectively:
  - Use quotes for exact phrases: "marketing agencies in Austin"
  - Use site: to search a specific website: site:linkedin.com "bakery owner"
  - Use filters: Tools → Past year to find recent information

Verify your sources:
  Don't take one result as definitive. Cross-check important facts
  with 2-3 sources. Accuracy matters more than speed in this work.

Keep a system:
  As you research, log everything directly into your spreadsheet.
  Don't try to remember things — record as you go.

----------------------------------------------------------------
SECTION 5: AVOIDING MISTAKES — ACCURACY IS EVERYTHING
----------------------------------------------------------------
In data entry, one mistake can cause real problems for a business
(a wrong email means a customer never gets their order confirmation).

Before delivering any work:
  - Re-read every cell for typos
  - Check that all columns are filled correctly
  - Sort the data and look for anything that stands out as wrong
  - Compare a sample of your work against the original source

It's better to take twice as long and deliver perfect work than
to rush and send something with errors.

----------------------------------------------------------------
SECTION 6: HOW TO PRICE YOUR WORK
----------------------------------------------------------------
Starting rates:

  Data entry / research:    $8–$15 per hour
  Flat project rate:        Estimate the hours, multiply by your rate

Example: If a client needs 200 rows of data entered and you can
do about 50 rows per hour, that's 4 hours × $10/hour = $40.
Quote a flat $40 project fee so the client knows the total upfront.

----------------------------------------------------------------
SECTION 7: WHERE TO FIND WORK
----------------------------------------------------------------
  Fiverr     — Create a gig for data entry or research services.
  Upwork     — Apply for data entry or virtual assistant jobs.
  Local      — Small businesses and sole traders often have paper
               records or disorganized spreadsheets they need cleaned up.

----------------------------------------------------------------
MINI PROJECT
----------------------------------------------------------------
Build a sample spreadsheet from a list of fake data.

Create a Google Sheet with at least 20 rows containing:
  - Business name
  - Owner name
  - Email address
  - Phone number
  - City
  - Date added

Make sure all data is consistently formatted and all columns are
clearly labeled. Share the link as a portfolio sample (set to
"Anyone with the link can view").

