LESSON 7: TUTORING & TEACHING ONLINE
================================================================

WHAT YOU'LL DO
--------------
Teach other students subjects you're good at over video call.
If you're strong in a subject, there are students who need help —
and parents willing to pay for it.

----------------------------------------------------------------
SECTION 1: WHAT ONLINE TUTORING LOOKS LIKE AS A TEEN
----------------------------------------------------------------
You meet with students over Zoom or Google Meet (video call) and
walk them through a subject they're struggling with.

Sessions are usually 30–60 minutes. The student comes with
questions, homework, or upcoming tests they want to prepare for.
Your job is to explain things clearly, at their level, in a way
their teacher hasn't been able to.

You don't need to be a perfect expert. You just need to be at
least 1-2 grade levels ahead of the student and able to explain
things simply.

----------------------------------------------------------------
SECTION 2: PICKING YOUR SUBJECT
----------------------------------------------------------------
Choose what you're genuinely good at — not just what you think
sounds impressive.

Good tutoring subjects for teens:

  Math           — Algebra, geometry, pre-calculus, basic statistics
  Sciences       — Biology, chemistry, physics basics
  English        — Essay writing, grammar, reading comprehension
  History        — Exam prep, essay structure for history class
  Coding         — Scratch, Python basics, HTML/CSS
  Test prep      — SAT/ACT math and reading sections
  Languages      — If you speak a second language fluently

Start with one subject. It's better to be known as "the great
math tutor" than someone who claims to teach everything.

----------------------------------------------------------------
SECTION 3: HOW TO RUN A TUTORING SESSION ON ZOOM OR GOOGLE MEET
----------------------------------------------------------------
Before the session:
  - Ask the student (or their parent) what specifically they
    need help with
  - Review the topic yourself so it's fresh in your mind
  - Prepare 1-2 example problems you can walk through together

During the session:
  - Start by asking what they're struggling with most
  - Explain the concept first, then work through an example
    together step by step
  - Let them try a practice problem while you watch
  - Give feedback and correct mistakes gently
  - Leave time at the end to answer any remaining questions

Use the screen share feature to show your working or to view
the student's work together.

After the session:
  - Send a short note summarizing what was covered
  - Suggest 1-2 things to practice before the next session

----------------------------------------------------------------
SECTION 4: MAKING A SIMPLE LESSON PLAN
----------------------------------------------------------------
You don't need a formal lesson plan for a tutoring session.
But having a rough outline keeps you on track.

A simple session plan:

  Minutes 0-5     Welcome and find out what they need today
  Minutes 5-15    Explain the key concept(s)
  Minutes 15-30   Work through example problems together
  Minutes 30-45   Student attempts problems independently,
                  you help where they get stuck
  Minutes 45-55   Review, answer questions
  Minutes 55-60   Recap and set practice for next time

Adjust based on how the student is doing. If a concept clicks
faster than expected, move on. If they're stuck, slow down.

----------------------------------------------------------------
SECTION 5: HOW TO EXPLAIN THINGS CLEARLY
----------------------------------------------------------------
Three techniques that make explanations land:

  Use examples before rules
    Don't start with the formula — start with a problem.
    "Here's a question. Let's figure it out together."
    The rule becomes obvious after working through the example.

  Ask "does that make sense?" often
    Check in throughout. Students often pretend to understand
    when they're lost. Create an environment where it's okay
    to say "I still don't get it."

  Use analogies
    Connect new concepts to things the student already knows.
    "Think of variables like boxes that hold a number —
    whatever number we put in the box, x becomes that."

If a student doesn't understand your explanation, try a
completely different approach. Good tutors have multiple ways
to explain the same thing.

----------------------------------------------------------------
SECTION 6: HOW TO PRICE YOUR WORK
----------------------------------------------------------------
Starting rates:

  Elementary school subjects:   $10–$15 per hour
  Middle school subjects:       $12–$18 per hour
  High school subjects:         $15–$25 per hour
  Test prep (SAT/ACT):          $20–$30 per hour

Offer a free 15-minute intro session. It lets the student and
parent decide if you're a good fit with no risk.

----------------------------------------------------------------
SECTION 7: WHERE TO FIND STUDENTS
----------------------------------------------------------------
  School community   — Post on school bulletin boards, ask teachers
                       if they can mention you to struggling students,
                       or post in school Facebook/WhatsApp groups.

  Facebook groups    — Local community or parent groups. Post:
                       "I'm a [grade] student offering [subject]
                       tutoring for [lower grades] at $X/hour."

  Wyzant             — A tutoring platform where students find tutors.
                       Takes a fee but brings clients to you.

  Word of mouth      — Once your first student sees results, they'll
                       tell others. One good review or recommendation
                       can bring in 3-4 more clients.

